Ranger is actually my favorite character for a variety of reasons.
They're fast, stealthy and fairly smart; they detect traps almost
instantly; if elven (which I always choose) they have infrared vision;
they have good ways to attack floating eyes, cockatrices, exploding
spheres, and so forth without experiencing the bad effects of fighting
them in melee combat. Heck, they can even sometimes kill yellow lights
without getting blinded!
On the downside, of course, they're rather lightweight, have no good
skill class for a melee weapon, and can get killed rather quickly by
any monster that grabs you and strangles you (such as an owlbear,
carnivorous ape, python, etc.)
The reason I really like Rangers is that they require you to think
before moving, not just charge through a dungeon killing everything in
sight.

Some strategies I've discovered follow: (you may already have realized
these things for yourself, or not):

- Always play an elf. You'll be chaotic (which I enjoy) but you'll
have infrared vision. Infrared is invaluable for a Ranger (especially
in the Gnomish Mines).
- First move of the game: #name your elven dagger Sting. 
- Do NOT speed through dark passageways. You can see almost any
approaching monster, so take your time. If something nasty is
approaching, wield your bow and shoot it. You get several shots to
every move of the monster's (unless it's really fast) so chances are
you can kill it before it gets to you. Even if it DOES get right next
to you without dying, you can just go ahead and keep shooting arrows
(they're just as deadly at close range!)
- NEVER allow yourself to get burdened (or only for very brief periods
of time.) You're not a great fighter. Often your speed will be all
that saves you.
If you are burdened and find yourself in a fight, drop lots of stuff
right away. Don't even think twice -- just do it.
- Wield your Sting most of the time. Then, when a pack of hill orcs
poofs into existence over in the next room, you'll know about it ahead
of time and be able to choose the best place for your battle. Pick a
nice, long hallway, position yourself at one end and wait. As soon as
the first orc appears at the end of the hallway, switch to bow and
begin shooting. With any luck they'll just charge into your line of
fire and die before they get near you.
- Two things to avoid: getting surrounded and "grabby" monsters.
Getting surrounded is a very bad idea for obvious reasons: you have no
good weapon for melee combat. "Grabby" monsters, like owlbears,
carnivorous apes, etc. are very bad news for a ranger. If you find one
next to you, back away; you're faster than it, so you should be able
to gain a few squares, enough time to switch to bow and start
shooting. If one grabs you, kill it ASAP, with a wand if necessary.
- Do the Gnomish Mines before the main dungeon branch. Many people say
that the Gnomish Mines are more dangerous than the main branch, but I
find the opposite is true for a ranger. The main danger is from traps,
which you can detect. In fact, half the traps are a blessing rather
than a curse, because they give you a chance to restock on your
ammunition! Find and #untrap every arrow trap you come across. Also,
start collecting daggers. As a Ranger you can throw four daggers per
round. Gnomes usually go down with one dagger, gnome kings take two or
three, dwarfs three or four -- and that takes care of 90% of the
monsters in the Mines right there.
(I usually find that, somehow, my dog almost never survives the
Gnomish Mines. So you might want to check out the first few levels of
the main dungeon branch for shops first before going into the Mines.)
- Weight is a big problem. Your strength is low, and you can't afford
to become burdened. Keep only what you need, and make a lot of little
stashes. Dragon scale mail and a BoH are high-priority wishes. The
first time you kill a monster with a dwarvish or elven mithril coat
(quite common in the Mines), grab it and put it on. It offers the best
compromise between weight and protection until you get the dragon
scale mail.
- Use ranged weapons to your best advantage, to take out cockatrices,
yellow lights, exploding spheres, etc. from a distance.
- Don't worry about getting reflection; that will come automatically
with your Quest Artifact (Longbow of Diana). Get the Longbow ASAP;
then you won't have to worry about carrying around ammo anymore! (It
creates arrows when #invoked.) Get Scorpius from a distance, with a
wand of fire or something similar. (I did this the last time I fought
him; it took only three blasts. The whole time he sat on the up stair
staring at me stupidly.)

